A||||6||Clint||Code 44 problems HELP!||||1||
Z||000000||Clint||12-08-1999||01:34 PM||||Anyone have any ideas on this one?<BR>Here’s the situation.<BR>2 weekends ago I decided to crawl into the back of my car and fix the problems it’s had since I bought it.  It has had an error code of Low Coolant voltage for quite sometime and I knew one of the coolant sensor was wrong.  The gas mileage was around 23-25, which is low for a 4cyl stick.<P>I got into it and found that the coolant sensor on the top of the block was not only wrong, someone had butchered the wiring harness, cut the plug off and connected one of it’s wires to the lone terminal on this new sensor.  I replaced the sensor with one from a spare car and cut the plug off a spare harness and installed it.  BINGO my gas mileage goes up to 32 and the coolant gauge on the dash starts to read correctly.<P>While I was there I also noticed another sensor was cut and it’s lone wire was grounded to the frame.  It was an oval shaped plug.  I fixed this as well.  Since then I have been led to believe it is an anti-knock sensor.<P>THE PROBLEM:<BR>After doing all this and the noticeable improvement that came with it I received a new error code of 44.  This only happened once or twice and I ignored it since the car was running good (although had a little less power)  after a week though the error started to occur more frequently, then the car started to buck and kick on me while driving.  I immediately noticed a drop in gas mileage, like to 10 MPG.  At first I though plug wires.  So I replaced all of them with the set from my spare Fiero.  No good.  Next I pulled the plugs.  They were black from running WAY to rich.  Since I was getting a lean exhaust error, I figured the ECM thought the car was going to burn down so it has been trying to richen the mix. (this also explains the bucking and kicking at speeds, it was probably flooding out).  Since the ECM was seeing a lean exhaust and I had evidence it was not, I decided the O2 sensor was bad.  I replaced it last night.<P>I’m still getting the error (although it runs better now). Could that used coolant sensor I installed be part of the problem?  Did I get a bad O2 sensor?<P>Thoughts anyone?<BR>Clint <BR>84 Fiero Coupe (just got needs work) Automatic 4cyl<BR>85 Fiero Coupe (daily driver) 5-speed 4cyl <BR>85 Fiero SE (project/spare) 5-speed 4cyl <BR>85 Fiero Coupe (Parts) 5-speed 4cyl<BR>Anyone want to buy a Car?<BR>Bradford County PA <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Voyd||12-08-1999||01:59 PM||||when i bought my car it had a code 44 in it. it was the EGR valve that was bad and needed immediate replacing. fixed it and everything is fine now. check it out, cant hurt.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||12-09-1999||02:09 AM||||Clint, <P>The duke used in the 84 never came with a knock sensor (don't know about the 87-88).  I think I know what sensor you're talking about, just can't remember the name.  Where's it at?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Clint||12-09-1999||08:06 AM||||All, it appears to be the coolant sensor.  I replaced it last night and noticed significant improvement.<P>It's and 85, not and 84.  I don't know whether the 84 had a knock sensor either.  I can look tonight and see if it has the same sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||12-09-1999||09:00 AM||||Not real sure about this, but I think that knock sensors were only on the 4 cyls with DIS.  I dont believe that the earlier 4 cyls had a knock sensor.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Clint||12-10-1999||10:06 AM||||after looking for sensors at the parts store i now believe this sensor to be the actualy fan switch.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||12-10-1999||11:02 PM||||There are three sensors that measure coolant temperature. 1) Gauge sending unit, 2) ECM sensor, and 3) coolant fan switch.||||reg||1
